## OIDC

Full OAuth 2.1 authentication using an external identity provider. Supports user login flows, SSO, and multi-user access control. Which of the two OIDC modes runs follows from which variables are set; `{{ env_prefix }}_AUTH_MODE` states the choice instead of leaving it to be inferred. Which mode to choose is decided on the OIDC page's [Which mode](oidc.md#which-mode); the provider pages are on [OIDC providers](oidc-providers.md).

### How remote mode works

The client authenticates with the identity provider and presents the resulting token, which the server validates locally against the provider's JWKS:

```
Client → OIDC Provider (log in, get JWT)
Client → {{ project_name }} (present JWT, validated via JWKS)
```

No redirect passes through the server and no code exchange happens, so the provider needs no client registered for this server.

### How oidc-proxy mode works

The server proxies OIDC itself, with no external auth sidecar to deploy:

```
Client → {{ project_name }} → OIDC Provider
```

1. Client connects to the server
2. Server redirects to the OIDC provider for login
3. Provider authenticates the user and returns a code
4. Server exchanges the code for tokens
5. Subsequent requests include the JWT

### Remote mode variables

`{{ env_prefix }}_BASE_URL` and `{{ env_prefix }}_OIDC_CONFIG_URL`, plus any of the optional variables below. Omitting the two client credentials is what selects this mode.

